{"prompt": "A shirt costs $25 after a 20% discount was applied twice. What was the original price?", "response": "Let me work through this carefully. Two successive 20% discounts means the price was multiplied by 0.8 twice: original x 0.8 x 0.8 = original x 0.64. So original = 25 / 0.64 = 39.0625. Check: 39.0625 x 0.8 = 31.25, and 31.25 x 0.8 = 25. Correct. The original price was $39.06 (to the cent)."}
{"prompt": "If all Bloops are Razzies and no Razzies are Lazzies, can a Bloop be a Lazzie?", "response": "Step 1: every Bloop is a Razzie. Step 2: no Razzie is a Lazzie, so the sets Razzie and Lazzie are disjoint. Step 3: since Bloops sit entirely inside Razzies, and Razzies never overlap Lazzies, Bloops cannot overlap Lazzies either. So no — a Bloop cannot be a Lazzie."}
{"prompt": "A rectangle has perimeter 40 and area 96. What are its side lengths?", "response": "Let the sides be a and b. Perimeter gives 2(a+b) = 40, so a+b = 20. Area gives ab = 96. So I need two numbers that add to 20 and multiply to 96. Let me set up a quadratic: if a+b=20 and ab=96, then a and b are roots of x² - 20x + 96 = 0. Factoring: (x-12)(x-8) = x² - 20x + 96. So the sides are 12 and 8. Verify: 2(12+8) = 40 ✓ and 12×8 = 96 ✓."}
{"prompt": "You have 3 boxes. One contains only apples, one contains only oranges, and one contains both. All labels are wrong. You pick one fruit from the box labeled \"Apples & Oranges\" and it is an apple. Which box contains what?", "response": "Step 1: All labels are wrong, so the box labeled \"Apples & Oranges\" does NOT contain both fruits — it contains only one type. Step 2: I pulled an apple from it, so this box contains only apples. Step 3: Now consider the box labeled \"Oranges.\" Since all labels are wrong, it cannot contain only oranges. It also cannot contain only apples (that is already identified). So it must contain the mixed fruits. Step 4: The remaining box, labeled \"Apples,\" must therefore contain only oranges. Answer: \"Apples & Oranges\" label → only apples; \"Oranges\" label → mixed; \"Apples\" label → only oranges."}
{"prompt": "Three pipes can fill a tank in 6 hours, 9 hours, and 12 hours respectively. If all three are opened together, how long will it take to fill the tank?", "response": "Let me work with rates per hour. Pipe 1 fills 1/6 of the tank per hour. Pipe 2 fills 1/9 per hour. Pipe 3 fills 1/12 per hour. Combined rate = 1/6 + 1/9 + 1/12. Finding a common denominator (36): 1/6 = 6/36, 1/9 = 4/36, 1/12 = 3/36. So combined = 6/36 + 4/36 + 3/36 = 13/36 of the tank per hour. Time to fill = 1 ÷ (13/36) = 36/13 hours. That is approximately 2.77 hours, or 2 hours and 46 minutes."}
{"prompt": "In a certain code, CLOCK is written as KCOLC. How is WATCH written in the same code?", "response": "Let me analyze the pattern. CLOCK → KCOLC. Looking at the letters: C-L-O-C-K becomes K-C-O-L-C. It appears the word is being reversed but with a twist. Let me check: the reverse of CLOCK would be KCOLC. Yes, that matches exactly! So the encoding is simply reversing the word. Applying the same rule to WATCH: reversing W-A-T-C-H gives H-C-T-A-W. So WATCH is written as HCTAW."}
{"prompt": "A train travels 60 km in the first hour, then 80 km in the second hour, then 50 km in the third hour. What is its average speed over the entire trip?", "response": "Average speed is total distance divided by total time. Total distance = 60 + 80 + 50 = 190 km. Total time = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 hours. Average speed = 190/3 ≈ 63.33 km/h. Note: this is not the average of the three speeds (60+80+50)/3 = 63.33 — in this case it happens to be the same because each segment took the same amount of time, but that would not be true if the times were different."}
